{"chunk_id": "42dd6c0d642e", "text": "This section continues the burning of the Khandava forest. A divine voice reveals that Krishna and Arjuna are the ancient sages Nara and Narayana, making them unconquerable even to the gods. Realizing this, Indra abandons the battle and retreats with the celestials. With no opposition left, Krishna and Arjuna aid Agni as the forest burns, Arjuna slaughtering countless creatures with unstoppable skill while Krishna destroys attacking demons with his discus. During the destruction, the Asura architect Maya begs Arjuna for protection; Arjuna grants him refuge, and both Krishna and Agni spare him. Agni devours the forest for fifteen days, leaving only a few survivors. The narrative then shifts as King Janamejaya asks why certain birds survived, leading into the story of the ascetic Mandapala and the importance of progeny in fulfilling spiritual obligations.\nKrishna and Arjuna defeat the gods aiding Agni\u2019s burning of Khandava forest; Maya is spared by Arjuna, and a new tale begins explaining why certain birds survived.\nThemes: divine power, destruction and fate, mercy and protection, cosmic duty, progeny and spiritual debt", "vector": [0.002532297046855092, -0.019386904314160347, -0.01149002555757761, 0.025044770911335945, -0.0016088717384263873, 0.022685091942548752, -0.03520747646689415, -0.010819662362337112, -0.014747991226613522, -0.014466438442468643, 0.009432010352611542, 0.015485391020774841, 0.01061855349689722, -0.0066500031389296055, -0.008392947725951672, 0.00844657700508833, 0.04427078738808632, -0.008607463911175728, 0.02090192586183548, -0.01638367772102356, -0.015552426688373089, 0.01248216349631548, -0.01352122612297535, 0.002421687124297023, 7.876767631387338e-05, 0.024991141632199287, 0.006931555923074484, -0.029013320803642273, 0.006448894273489714, 0.0013415644643828273, -5.252924529486336e-05, -0.005486923269927502, 0.012790529988706112, -0.010404037311673164, -0.029442353174090385, -0.002624472137540579,